  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nAfternoon highs in the mid 80s to low 90s persist through Tuesday as \nan upper level ridge builds aloft and gradually slides eastward. The \nwarming trend culminates on Tuesday, with most locations looking to \ntop out in the 90s (80s in the higher terrain and portions of SE \nNM). Meanwhile, lows tomorrow night through Tuesday night are \nforecast to generally fall into the 60s to low 70s. Overall, \ntemperatures through early next week will run roughly 10 degrees \nabove normal, accompanied by mostly dry conditions. However, the \nweather pattern begins to shift early Tuesday, as an upper level \ntrough approaches our region from the Rockies. Models show moisture \nfrom what is now Hurricane Simon, off the Pacific coast of Mexico, \nbecoming entrained into southwesterly flow aloft as early as Monday \nnight. This brings low (10-15%) rain chances to our westernmost \ncounties and SE NM Monday night. The remnants of Simon continue to \nphase into the upper trough Tuesday into Wednesday. All the while, a \nshortwave within the upper level trough rolls over the Rockies into \nthe central Plains, dragging a surface trough along with it. \nIncreased moisture and lift from the slow-moving trough bring 10-40% \nrain chances over most of the region Tuesday evening through \nThursday. The best rain chances will tend to be collocated with the \nsurface trough. Depending on the exact track of this storm system, \nanother hazard to monitor may be the potential for high winds within \nthe Guadalupe/Delaware Mountains. Of course, details with this\nsystem will become clearer over the next few days. Otherwise, \nlowering highs bring a return of highs predominantly in the 80s \nby Thursday.",
